     "parsed_output": null,
     "text": "Round 1 used 4096-element tiles and ran in 21.8 \u00b5s. That moves about 126 MB, roughly 5.8 TB/s against the measured 6.5 TB/s peak. This round doubles the tile to 8192 elements, an untested guess at what helps here.\n\n- **Fewer blocks:** each block now moves twice the bytes, and the grid shrinks from 5120 to 2560 blocks. That is about 17 blocks per SM, so the last partial wave of blocks costs little.\n- **Contract unchanged:** the add stays in fp16, and there is still one launch over the data. The loads are zero-padded and the store is clipped, so no element at index `>= n` is read or written for any `n`.\n\n```python title=\"impl_cutile.py\"\nimport torch\nimport cuda.tile as ct\n\nConstInt = ct.Constant[int]\n\n# Fixed configuration literal\n_TILE = 8192\n\n\n@ct.kernel\ndef _vector_add_kernel(x, y, out, TILE: ConstInt):\n    bid = ct.bid(0)\n    # Bounds-padded loads for a possible partial last tile.\n    a = ct.load(x, (bid,), (TILE,), padding_mode=ct.PaddingMode.ZERO)\n    b = ct.load(y, (bid,), (TILE,), padding_mode=ct.PaddingMode.ZERO)\n    # Add in the input dtype (fp16), exactly like the reference.\n    c = a + b\n    # Bounds-clipped store: elements beyond n are dropped.\n    ct.store(out, (bid,), c)\n\n\ndef run(x, y):\n    out = torch.empty_like(x)\n    n = x.numel()\n    if n == 0:\n        return out\n    grid = (ct.cdiv(n, _TILE),)\n    ct.launch(torch.cuda.current_stream(), grid, _vector_add_kernel,\n              (x, y, out, _TILE))\n    return out\n\n\ndef get_last_config() -> dict:\n    return {\"TILE\": 8192}\n```",
     "type": "text"
    }
